From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Scope service tokens to the bound title

A launcher that binds the service to a Store product now also passes the
title's identity from its MicrosoftGame.config: XODUS_TITLE_ID (decimal, empty
when the config names no title) and XODUS_TITLE_CLIENT_ID (MSAAppId). A bound
service mints XSTS tokens only under that title identity, or with no title
claim at all (the runtime's own sign-in lookup asks that way, and such a token
carries the user and device only), with the relying party taken from the
endpoint list. It signs licence-token requests as that title whatever TitleId
and TitleClientId they carry.

Licence tokens are issued for the bound product and for products whose own
Store catalog entry names it as their game: add-ons and consumables
(addOnParent) and editions (Parent, Extends), looked up once per
product in the market-neutral display catalog. Bundles and storefronts the
game merely lists are not admitted, since they can grant other titles, and
neither is a product that names the game only as where it is sold
(SellableBy). A product the catalog does not confirm is refused and the
reason logged. A bound service refuses a relying party chosen by the
request, and one with a title binding but no product refuses licence
requests.

The licensing audience stays with the licence path: where the Player is the
Store account, a title's own XSTS request that resolves to the audience the
licence service uses is refused. A bound service also answers no MSA token
requests, which the runtime never makes. The service lists
title-scoped-tokens among its capabilities so a launcher can tell it apart
from one that ignores the title variables. Unbound services behave as
before, and a service bound without the title variables keeps the title a
request names.

